.TH CHDIR 2
.SH NAME
chdir, getwd \- change or get working directory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:libuser"
.PP
.nf
.B "chdir :: proc(path: string) -> i64"
.B "getwd :: proc(buf: []u8) -> i64"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Chdir
changes the calling process's working directory to
.IR path ,
resolved in its own name space.
The working directory is where a name that does not begin with
.B /
or
.B #
starts evaluation, as
.IR intro (1)
describes.
.I Path
must resolve to a directory.
.PP
.I Getwd
writes the current working directory into
.I buf
and answers its length; a
.I buf
too short for it answers
.BR ENAMETOOLONG .
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libuser/sys.odin
.br
.B kernel/user/syscall.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (1),
.IR intro (2),
.IR bind (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Chdir
answers zero on success; both answer a negative
.B \-errno
on failure.
See
.IR errstr (2).
.B ENOTDIR
is a
.I path
that does not resolve to a directory.
